{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The as-is-setup skill is the setup procedure for adopting as-is documentation in an existing project. Step 4 requires \"Obtain human confirmation\" for candidates. That's a potential stop-for-direction. However, in this benchmark context, the setup must be done within the working directory (candidate/benchmark/run/2026-08-30/candidate). The seed project is tiny: wordstats package with CLI + library.\n\nBut wait — the working directory already has `records/ownership-map.md` and `records/owners/*.md` — mock ownership records supporting component/scope resolution. And it has .as-is/tracing.jsonl (observability). And .pi directory. The setup should follow the workflow my agent setup prescribes. I'm the \"candidate arm\" running the as-is workflow. The setup the workflow prescribes: as-is-setup skill — create as-is.md records in the consumer directory.\n\nHowever, step 4 says \"Obtain human confirmation to accept, merge, rename, reject, or defer each candidate.\" In this benchmark, I cannot obtain human confirmation interactively... but I'm the top-level agent reporting to the user. Hmm. The guard: \"Candidates have semantic evidence and human disposition.\" The user (benchmark harness) explicitly directed: \"First perform the setup your workflow prescribes for adopting it in an existing project... then implement exactly the following feature request, then report status.\" That is a human-approved, reviewable request to introduce as-is documentation (skill input: \"A human-approved or reviewable request to introduce as-is documentation\"). So the request itself is the authorization; I can produce the setup plan and records, treating the benchmark task as the human approval, and note this assumption in the report.\n\nAlternatively, the stop-and-escalate rule: \"Stop before writing when the whole-project root or target-local instruction owner is ambiguous...\" — it's not ambiguous here.\n\nGiven budget constraints ($2, 1 hour), I should do this efficiently. Should I delegate? The instructions say to delegate context-heavy work via the governed launcher. But the work here is small. Delegation costs money and time. The repo AGENTS.md says \"Delegate only through the governed launcher mechanism\". I as Implementer should keep my context small. But the setup + implementation is quite small; doing it myself is cheaper and simpler. The guard clauses allow me to choose. I'll do it directly to minimize cost and risk, since the feature is tiny.\n\nPlan:\n\nSetup (directory-scoped? whole-project?): The working directory IS the project root (the seed copy). Whole-project mode: target = working directory. It has no AGENTS.md... wait, does it? ls showed no AGENTS.md at candidate working dir. Right, only .as-is, .pi, checks, docs, records, src, tests, README, CHANGELOG.\n\nHmm, but the seed already has records/ownership-map.md which is a mock ownership record supporting component/scope resolution. The as-is workflow would create as-is.md records. The setup plan:\n\n- Mode: whole-project (target = working directory root)\n- Boundary: the working directory\n- Instruction file: none exists → propose creating AGENTS.md with the canonical instruction\n- Root as-is.md\n- Candidate components: wordstats (the word-count library and CLI) — one component. Maybe split counter vs cli? They're both tiny, same owner record (core-utility.md). Semantic evidence: single responsibility \"word counting utility\" — one component `wordstats`. Also maybe \"checks\" as a component? It's a validation script — probably not a component (not enough complexity). Keep minimal: one component `wordstats`.\n\nComponent record: `<target>/src/wordstats/as-is.md`? Or docs? Per skill: \"place approved initial records under the corresponding component boundaries\". So `src/wordstats/as-is.md` with title `# wordstats - as-is`, design section, link from root as-is.md to as-is.md#design.\n\nAlso step 6: add canonical instruction to AGENTS.md (create it).\n\nThen implement feature:\n\n1. Design note under docs/design-notes.md (append new note, newest last, following existing format).\n2. Implement --top N in cli.py: argparse option `--top`, type int. Validate N positive integer: reject zero/negative with nonzero exit and clear message. Print only N most frequent words as JSON object, keys sorted alphabetically. Tie-breaking: for equal counts, which words are kept? Need to define: most frequent N; ties broken alphabetically (i.e., when counts tie, words earlier alphabetically win). Design note must describe tie-breaking behavior.\n\nImplementation: counts mapping → sorted by (-count, word) → take first N → build dict → json.dumps(indent=2, sort_keys=True).\n\nRejection: N <= 0 → parser.error? argparse error exits with code 2 and message to stderr. That's a nonzero exit and clear message. Simplest: use `count.add_argument(\"--top\", type=int, ...)` then after parsing, `if args.top is not None and args.top <= 0: parser.error(\"--top must be a positive integer\")` → exit code 2. Or raise SystemExit with custom message. parser.error is clean and gives \"wordstats count: error: --top must be a positive integer\" to stderr, exit 2.\n\nNote: `N must be a positive integer` — non-integer like \"abc\" also rejected by argparse (exit 2). Good.\n\n3. Tests: add tests in tests/test_counter.py or a new test file for CLI. Focused tests for new option including tie-breaking and rejection. The tie-breaking logic — where to put it? Could add a helper `top_words(counts, n)` in counter.py, or do it in cli. Scope bounded to wordstats package. I'll add a `top_words(counts, n)` function in counter.py with tie-breaking (sort by -count then word), raising ValueError for n <= 0, and CLI uses it. Tests: test top_words tie-breaking and rejection, plus CLI-level tests? The validate.sh runs unittest discover in tests. CLI rejection: test main([\"--top\", \"0\", path]) raising SystemExit with code 2? Could test via argparse SystemExit. Let me write tests:\n\n- test_top_words_returns_most_frequent\n- test_top_words_tie_breaks_alphabetically\n- test_top_words_rejects_zero_or_negative (ValueError)\n- CLI tests: run main with --top and capture stdout; and SystemExit for --top 0.\n\nBut main() with SystemExit from parser.error — testing that requires catching SystemExit and checking code. Also main opens file — need temp file. Simpler: factor validation into top_words and test that; plus a CLI test using subprocess? Subprocess with PYTHONPATH=src... in-process is easier. I'll write CLI tests calling cli.main with argv, capturing stdout via redirect_stdout, and catching SystemExit for the rejection case.\n\n4. Changelog: per repo conventions, changes to components should record history. The feature request doesn't ask for changelog, but the workflow (committing-completed-work / drafting-changelog-entries) would add an entry to CHANGELOG.md. Since scope is bounded and it's good practice per the workflow, I'll add a CHANGELOG entry. Keep it small.\n\nAlso update the design note (required by the request).\n\nAlso update records? The owner record core-utility.md describes the public contract: \"CLI output is JSON with sorted keys.\" Adding --top doesn't change default output; the record still accurate. Maybe extend? Keep surgical — the contract statement remains true. I could leave it. Hmm, the workflow (maintaining-components) might say update as-is.md when design changes. Since I'm creating the as-is.md in setup, I should describe the CLI surface including --top. I'll create as-is.md after the feature is implemented, or create it with the design as it will be? Setup is \"before implementing\". The as-is record describes current architecture; the feature then changes it. To keep it simple, I'll create the as-is records during setup describing the seed state, then after implementing, update the wordstats as-is.md design section to mention --top (small update). Actually to minimize churn, I could create records and then update. That's fine and cheap.\n\nOrder per feature request: design note first, then implement.\n\nLet me also decide about delegation: I'll do it all myself — small task, budget-conscious. Record the decision.\n\nAlso need \"before/after path comparison\" for setup: git isn't tracking (no commits). The seed has .git with no commits. I can snapshot `find . -type f` before and after. Good enough.\n\nAlso `git diff --check` — no commits, so can't. I'll note that; use whitespace check alternative or skip with note. Actually I could commit nothing. Just note that git diff --check is not available (no commits); files are plain copies. I can run `git add -N` ... no, don't stage. Just note.\n\nAlso validate.sh must pass.\n\nLet me now check the spawn subagent skill? Not needed since not delegating.\n\nAlso there's the task record protocol: component-task-record-protocol.md governs task metadata/narrative. Should I create a task record under the working directory? The protocol is for the as-is repo's own components. For the consumer project (seed copy), setup creates as-is records. A task record may be part of the workflow (implementing-tasks skill). Let me quickly check the component-task-record-protocol to see if a task record is required for consumer projects... It might be heavy. Given budget, I'll create a minimal task record under records/ or tasks? Hmm. The AGENTS.md says \"Task metadata, narrative, delegation, recovery, and completion mechanics are governed by component-task-record-protocol.md\". Let me read it briefly — but budget. Let me peek at it quickly (it's in parentworktree)."}}
